course: everyday
lesson: 66
title: How pets feel and saying goodbye
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Pets cannot tell us in words that they are ill, hurt or sad, so we watch for changes: a pet that hides much more than usual, snaps when it used to be friendly, or a bird sitting fluffed up and sleepy in the day may be ill.
explain: A sudden change in how a pet behaves is a message to tell an adult, who can take it to a vet; sudden grumpiness is often a sign of pain.
explain: Pets also have good feelings: they bond with their families, miss them when alone, and get bored without toys, play and new things to sniff.
explain: All pets die one day, usually of old age or illness. Feeling sad, crying and talking about it are natural, because grief is how we feel when we lose someone we love.
explain: When her old dog died, a girl in Texas thought it was her fault for not noticing sooner. She told her mum, who explained the dog had simply grown old, and together they planted a flower in his memory.
explain: A girl in Texas did not notice her old dog had stopped eating and was hiding; by the time the vet saw him, his illness was much harder to treat.
explain: A boy in Dublin was told his cat had just gone away when it died; he waited at the window for weeks, and he felt worse when he learnt the truth later.
fact: An animal that hides more than usual may be ill, in pain or frightened.
fact: A pet that suddenly snaps when it used to be friendly is often in pain, so you stop touching it and tell an adult.
fact: A bird that sits fluffed up and sleepy in the daytime may be sick.
fact: Pets bond with their families and miss them when they are alone.
fact: Bored animals can become unhappy and destructive, so they need toys, play, walks and new things to sniff.
fact: Crying and feeling sad when a pet dies is natural, and talking with your family helps.
fact: Drawing a picture, writing a letter, planting a flower or holding a small ceremony helps you remember a pet.
fact: When an animal is very ill and cannot get better, a vet can end its suffering painlessly; this is called putting it to sleep.
fact: Children often wrongly blame themselves when a pet dies; most pets die of old age or illness.
fact: A new pet does not replace one that died, so a family waits until it is ready.
fact: A pet buried in a garden needs a deep enough grave, so other animals cannot dig it up, and an adult's help; some places have rules, and a vet can suggest other choices.
fact: Animals often hide pain and illness, so changes in eating, sleeping or hiding can be warning signs.
fact: Being told the truth gently about a pet's death helps children grieve better than being told a story.
fact: People also grieve for people: «my other half» means a husband, wife or partner, the person someone loves most and shares life with.
fact: Divorce means a married couple end their marriage; a break-up means a couple stop being together, and the person left alone may feel lonely.
exercise: Your cat has been hiding under the bed much more than usual. What should you do? | Tell an adult, it may be ill or in pain | Pull it out and make it play with you | Ignore it, cats always hide | Put its food and water under the bed forever
exercise: Your friendly old dog suddenly snaps when you touch its back. What should you do? | Stop touching it and tell an adult | Tell it off for being naughty | Keep stroking the same place gently | Give it a hug to cheer it up
exercise: Why might a friendly pet suddenly become grumpy? | It may be in pain | It has stopped loving you | It wants more sweets | It is trying to be the boss
exercise: Your budgie sits fluffed up and sleepy in the middle of the day. What should you do? | Tell an adult, it may be sick | Let it sleep, it is just lazy | Shake the cage to wake it | Give it chocolate for energy
exercise: Your dog follows you everywhere when you come home from school. What is a kind response? | Greet it calmly and give it some attention | Push it away again and again until it stops | Shut it in another room | Shout at it loudly so that it calms down
exercise: Why does a dog follow its family around after they come home? | It has bonded with them and missed them | It is hungry and wants food, nothing else | It is angry they went out | It wants to guard the door from strangers
exercise: Your dog chews the cushions when left with nothing to do. What would help most? | Toys, play, walks and new things to sniff | Leaving it alone for even longer each day | Taking away all its toys | Feeding it twice as much at every meal
exercise: Your pet hamster died and you cannot stop crying. What is true? | It is okay to cry and to talk about it with your family | Crying over a pet is silly | You should hide your feelings from everyone, even your family | You must forget about it the very same day and move on fast
exercise: Which is a good way to say goodbye to a pet that died? | Plant a flower or write it a letter | Pretend it never lived | Throw away all its photos and toys at once | Get angry with your family about it
exercise: Your old cat is very ill and the vet talks about putting it to sleep. What can you do? | Ask your parents questions and say goodbye if you can | Run away and hide in your room until it is all over | Pretend you do not care | Ask the vet to keep it alive and ill for as long as possible
exercise: What does it mean when a vet puts an animal to sleep? | The vet ends its suffering painlessly when it cannot get better | The animal sleeps for a while and wakes up the next day well again | The vet gives it a very long nap | The animal is sent away to live on a farm in the countryside
exercise: A boy thinks his goldfish died because he forgot to say goodnight to it. What should he do? | Talk to an adult, who can help him understand | Keep the guilty secret to himself forever | Promise never to have a pet again | Blame his little sister for it instead of himself
exercise: A week after your dog died, someone offers you a new puppy. What is wise? | Wait until the family is ready | Take it at once so you forget the old dog | Give it the old dog's name so it is the same | Say no to ever having a pet again
exercise: Your family wants to bury your rabbit in the garden. What should you do? | Ask an adult and dig deep enough | Dig a tiny hole near the surface | Bury it in the park without asking | Leave it in its hutch
exercise: Why should a pet's grave be deep enough? | A shallow grave can be dug up by animals | Deep graves keep the pet warm | Shallow graves are against the rules everywhere | The pet needs room to move
test: A girl in Cork notices her usually cuddly cat hisses when picked up and hides all day. What is the most likely reason? | It may be ill or in pain and needs a vet | It has suddenly decided that it does not like her | It is training to be a guard cat | It is just in a bad mood for fun today
test: A boy in Atlanta says that dogs do not have feelings, so it does not matter if his dog is alone all day. What is wrong with this? | Dogs bond with their families, miss them and get bored | Dogs only care about food, so they never miss anybody | Dogs prefer being alone | Nothing, he is right
test: Which of these pets most likely needs a vet? | A parrot sitting fluffed up and sleepy at noon | A cat stretching after a nap | A dog wagging its tail at the door when you come in | A rabbit doing a happy binky
test: A girl in Leeds feels terrible because her pet guinea pig died of old age. Which thought is true? | Most pets die of old age or illness, not because of a child | It must have been her fault for not looking after it well enough | She should never feel sad about pets | Guinea pigs never die of old age
test: A family's dog has a painful illness that cannot be cured. Why might the vet suggest putting it to sleep? | To end its suffering painlessly | To give it a rest before it gets better | Because the vet is too busy | Because old dogs must always be put to sleep
test: A week after their cat died, a family in Wellington makes a little garden with its name on a stone. How does this help? | Remembering a pet helps the sadness slowly get smaller | It brings the cat back | It makes them forget about the cat much faster than doing nothing | It stops anyone from feeling sad again
test: A child says a new kitten will be exactly the same as the cat that died. Which is true? | A new pet does not replace the old one; each is its own animal | A new kitten will remember the old cat's life | New pets always behave exactly like the old ones they come after | The old cat's name will change the kitten
test: A dog left alone with no toys has dug holes and torn up a cushion. What is it most likely feeling? | Bored and unhappy | Proud and happy | Hungry for cushions | Sleepy and calm
test: our cat keeps hiding under the bed all day, she never does that | She may be ill or in pain, tell an adult | She is just playing hide and seek with you | Cats always hide when they are happy | She wants you to pull her out and cuddle her
test: rex always so friendly but he snapped when i touched his leg | He may be in pain, stop and tell an adult | He is just being naughty, tell him off loudly | Touch it again so he gets used to it | He does not like you any more
test: hammy died n i think its my fault cos i forgot to say night 😭 | Not your fault, most pets die of old age or illness | Yes, pets die if you forget them | Yes, hamsters need a goodnight every single day or they get ill | It is your fault, so do not cry
test: can we just get a new dog tmrw, same kind, then i wont be sad | A new pet does not replace him, wait till you are ready | Yes, a new dog makes the sadness go away straight away | Yes, the same kind is the same dog | Yes, get one before you forget him
test: our cat's been hiding and won't eat for 2 days | Tell an adult, the cat may need a vet | Leave her, cats just do that | Give her loads more treats to tempt her out | Wait a week to see
test: my dog died yesterday and i keep crying | It is normal to be sad, talk about him with your family | Crying means you are weak | You should be over it by now, it was only a dog | Get a new dog today so you forget about him quick
test: Jake says animals always show when they hurt. right? | No, many animals hide pain | Yes, they always cry out | Yes, they limp every single time | Yes, unless they are cats
test: heard a dog snapped at a kid out of nowhere, it was always friendly, they're saying it's gone bad | Real lesson: a pet that suddenly snaps is often in pain, so stop touching it and tell an adult | Real lesson: friendly dogs can just turn bad for no reason at all, so they should all be kept away | It proves old dogs are dangerous | It is gossip, so nothing needs doing
test: my mate says when a vet puts a pet to sleep it's like a long nap and it wakes up later | Not true; putting to sleep means the vet ends its suffering painlessly, and it does not wake up | He is right, it wakes up next week | He is right, it is just a really deep nap and the pet comes home feeling better in a day or so | He is right, if it was young
test: Sophie cried all day cos her rabbit died and people are calling her a baby, should i join in | No; crying when a pet dies is natural, and she needs kindness | Yes, because it was only a rabbit and rabbits do not really count | Yes, so she cheers up | Yes, but only at lunchtime
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an animal hiding more than is usual? | It may be ill, in pain or frightened | It is merely playing a game | It is invariably content and well rested | It wishes to be picked up and carried
exam: Identify the meaning of a bird that sits fluffed up and sleepy during the day. | It may be sick | It is very happy | It is preparing to sing | It is about to lay an egg
exam: It being given that a child believes a pet died through his own fault, select the response consistent with the established facts. | Children often wrongly blame themselves, most pets dying of old age or illness | The child is usually correct in such belief, as pets depend entirely upon children | The child should keep the feeling secret | The child should get another pet at once
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning a bored animal. | It requires toys, play, walks and new things to explore | It requires more sleep only | It requires to be left alone in a dark room for extended periods | It requires a smaller cage
test: when Grandad died nan said "she's lost her other half", what's that mean | Grandad was her husband, the person she loved most, and she is very sad; be gentle and spend time with her | She lost half of something in the house, like half a cake or half a pair of gloves, and wants help finding it | She is angry with Grandad | It means she must find a new one quickly
test: overheard mum: "they're getting divorced and fighting over who keeps the dog" | The parents are ending their marriage and both want the dog; it is their business and the dog needs care either way | The dog is getting married | The dog will be taken by the police because grown-ups are fighting about it, and it will live in a police station | It means the dog bit someone
test: my cousin said Auntie got a puppy "cos she's lonely since the break-up" | Auntie's relationship ended and she feels lonely, so a pet keeps her company; do not tease her about it | Puppies cause break-ups | She broke something and the puppy is a gift | It means the puppy is broken
